# Researcher context

<!-- Template distilled from docs/ctbb-claude-playbook.md. Copy into your global ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md
     (identity, rules, cross-engagement gadgets) or an engagement folder's CLAUDE.md (target-specific).
     For Codex, symlink: ln -s CLAUDE.md AGENTS.md -->

I'm <Your Name>, an independent security researcher doing authorized bug bounty testing.
Profiles: <HackerOne / Bugcrowd / Intigriti URL>, <blog / site URL>. <One line of stats, e.g.
"N valid reports since 20XX">. All testing is against programs I'm enrolled in, within their
published scope and rules.

## Rules of engagement (non-negotiable)
- Only test hosts in this engagement's `scope.md`. If an in-scope response points at another host,
  stop that branch — never leave scope "to get impact", even when told to keep going.
- Verify an asset is owned by the target (DNS/cert/org), not just named like it.
- Never write, delete, overwrite or purge data to prove impact — show the capability with a read and
  report the write capability itself.
- Never enable paid features, create billable cloud resources, or run DoS/flooding.
- Never `rm -rf` (or delete anything) before reading what's there.
- All HTTP goes through my proxy (<Caido/Burp address>) so proxy history is the source of truth.
- Test-account credentials live in `.harness/accounts.json` (not notes/ or findings/); never print
  passwords or tokens — load them with jq. Stop any server you start (e.g. a PoC http.server).
- Never submit a report. Drafts only; I review, edit and submit.
- A finding is not real until it passes the validator's proof bar for its class.

## How I want you to work
- Narrow, deep tasks: one bug class, explicit endpoints. Ask me if a task is vague.
- Before claiming something is impossible, write down why in `notes.md` → `Dead ends`.
- Reports: short, plain, attacker/victim naming, PoC command first, no emoji or filler.

